# A SQL query with `=` and `<>` in WHERE: the rewrite uses `==` and `!=`.
mode: syntax
---- query
SELECT orderId FROM 'orders.csv' WHERE customerId = 'c-1' AND amount <> 0
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..74
  PIPELINE@0..73
    ERROR@0..73
      IDENT@0..6 "SELECT"
      WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
      IDENT@7..14 "orderId"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      IDENT@15..19 "FROM"
      WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
      STRING@20..32 "'orders.csv'"
      WHITESPACE@32..33 " "
      IDENT@33..38 "WHERE"
      WHITESPACE@38..39 " "
      IDENT@39..49 "customerId"
      WHITESPACE@49..50 " "
      EQ@50..51 "="
      WHITESPACE@51..52 " "
      STRING@52..57 "'c-1'"
      WHITESPACE@57..58 " "
      IDENT@58..61 "AND"
      WHITESPACE@61..62 " "
      IDENT@62..68 "amount"
      WHITESPACE@68..69 " "
      LTGT@69..71 "<>"
      WHITESPACE@71..72 " "
      LONG@72..73 "0"
  WHITESPACE@73..74 "\n"

error[DS0105]: `SELECT ... FROM` is SQL, not DataScript
  --> query:1:1
  |
1 | SELECT orderId FROM 'orders.csv' WHERE customerId = 'c-1' AND amount <> 0
  | ^^^^^^         ---- in DataScript the table comes first
  | |
  | this is SQL
  |
  = help: start with the table and pipe it into operators: `from 'orders.csv' | where customerId == 'c-1' and amount != 0 | project orderId`
